<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niğde</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Nigde</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Niğde is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, the capital of Niğde Province, lying between the volcanic landscape of Cappadocia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taurus-mountains">Taurus Mountains</a>. Nearby lie the remains of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyana">Tyana</a>, capital of the Neo-Hittite kingdom of Tuwana. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was an important town of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a>, which built its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaeddin-mosque">Alaeddin Mosque</a> in 1223, and it later passed to the Karamanids and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nigde.jpg" alt="Niğde" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nigde_Kale_1228.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
